  "query_preview": "..\n\nThrough next Monday...\n\nA dry zonal flow will bring continued warm & dry weather through\nFriday, with temps mostly 70s to lower 80s. There are a few \ndetails. A weak front arrives today bringing somewhat breezy NW \nwinds during the peak heating hours, which along with another day\nof low humidity will keep fire concerns elevated (though it won't\nbe as warm/dry/windy as Monday). Behind the front we will see a\ncouple days of easterly surface winds and thus slightly cooler\ntemps Wednesday & Thursday. Amplification ahead of a deepening low\nover BC will push temps/downslope winds up and humidity down on a\npre-frontal Friday. This is a day to watch with regard to fire \nweather, especially considering the stretch of warmth/dryness \nbetween now and then. On the positive side is the fact that the \ncold front is not expected to arrive until early Saturday (strong \nmodel consensus here). The delayed fropa will alleviate any \nfrontal wind concern during the heat of day, but it does open the \ndoor to potential mountain waves in the lee of the Beartooths and \nBighorns early Saturday. 700mb flow on the order of 30-35 knots \nsuggests western foothills gusts to 40 mph along the western \nfoothills Friday, then perhaps 30-40 mph erratic gusts into early \nSaturday if there are mountain waves. Something to watch.\n\nConfidence is high for a significant cool down this weekend. \nEnsembles show 850mb temps falling to near +10C by late Saturday \nafternoon (again, starting with an early morning fropa, high temps\nwill likely be early in the day), then near 0C by 12z Sunday. \nThis is where confidence ends. The aforementioned BC low is \nexpected to drop in some fashion to the PacNW Saturday and great \nbasin Sunday-Monday. Furthermore, remnants from hurricane Rachel \nare expected to lift thru the SW CONUS as the low over the west \ndeepens, potentially being a player in our region with its \nmoisture content. There is a lot of uncertainty here. If the low \nends up cutting off too far west and eventually south of us, \ndynamics for precip are weaker and our temps will be warmer. If \nthe low drives more aggressively to the northern great basin it \nmay shunt the subtropical moisture to our east, but precip chances\noverall will be greater. Taking a look at 06/12z cluster analyses\nthere is clearly very high spread, but it should be noted there is\nfairly high confidence in (1) much cooler temps and (2) a deep \nlow impacting a large portion of the western CONUS this weekend \ninto early next week. Details regarding potential impacts in our \nforecast area are impossible at this time. Mountain snow is \nlooking likely, and there is increasing potential for upslope snow\nfor our western foothills (looking at you, Melville). Lower \nelevations, especially the west half of our cwa, are at risk of \nseeing some wet snow accumulation as well (this includes \nBillings). If the upper low takes a track favorable for prolonged \ndynamic forcing in our region, a significant snowfall event is \npossible. It is also possible that forcing misses us and surface \ntemps remain too warm. Individual ensemble members show a very \nlarge spread in potential QPF and snowfall. For now, keep \nmonitoring the forecast, and please do not take seriously any \nspecific snowfall forecasts you may see so far in advance. Stay \ntuned.\n\nTaking a look at a few way-to-early NBM probabilities, the chance\nof 1+ inches of snow ranges from 10% over eastern valleys, to \n20-40% central lower elevations (including Billings) and 65-75%\nalong the western foothills. There are plenty of leaves on area\ntrees so the weight of any wet snow accumulation could be \nproblematic.\n\nThe low should continue to impact us in some fashion through \nMonday, before exiting to our east/south by the middle of next \nweek. As for temperatures, after the very warm week, look for \nfalling temps Saturday then below normal temps (highs in 30s to \nnear 50F, warmest east) Sunday & Monday.\n\nJKL",
